course: dictionary
lesson: 8880
title: Shouting and loud voices
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Shouting means saying something very loudly, with a big voice. The words around it show you why someone is shouting. «Training with shouting, smacking or fear can make dogs scared or aggressive» tells you shouting is something a dog trainer should not use, because it frightens dogs.
explain: Look for clues in the rest of the sentence. «Drowning can happen quickly and quietly, often without any shouting or splashing» shows shouting as a loud sound that a person in danger might make. The word quietly is the clue that the danger can be hard to notice.
explain: Shouting can be a verb (people shout) or a noun (the noise they make). In «a pIven (rooster) shouting at dawn», shouting is a verb, because the rooster is doing it. Try swapping in a word like calling loudly: if the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Shouting means saying something very loudly, using a big voice.
fact: People shout to call someone far away, to warn of danger, or because they are angry or excited.
fact: As a noun, shouting is the noise of people talking very loudly, as in «a shouting match».
fact: «Shouting at someone» usually means being angry with them, while «shouting to someone» means calling so they can hear you.
exercise: «Training with shouting, smacking or fear can make dogs scared or aggressive.» What does shouting mean here? | Singing a happy song | *Saying something very loudly | Giving a dog a treat | Quietly whispering a secret
exercise: «Many old dogs slowly lose their hearing, so hand signals and gentle touches work instead of shouting.» Why do trainers use hand signals instead of shouting? | *Because many old dogs cannot hear loud voices well | Because hand signals are louder | Because dogs like shouting best
exercise: In «Drowning can happen quickly and quietly, often without any shouting or splashing», what kind of word is shouting? | An adjective, a describing word | *A noun, a name for a loud noise | A verb, an action word | A question word
test: In «a pIven (rooster) shouting at dawn», what is the rooster doing? | Hiding from the farmer | Sleeping in the barn | *Calling out very loudly | Eating corn from the ground
test: Why might someone shout «Fire!»? | *To warn people of danger | To sing a song | To say goodnight to friends | To ask for a drink
exam: What does shouting for help mean? | Whispering a secret to a friend | Dancing with friends at a party | *Calling out loudly so that others come and help you | Sleeping quietly in a warm bed
